\documentclass{article}
\title{Nitrate Transport in a Shallow Coastal Aquifer}
\author{D. Ferrante and K. Owusu}
\date{June 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Nitrate leaching from fertilised plots threatens the shallow aquifer
that supplies the coastal villages.
We combine two years of well sampling with a transport model
to estimate how quickly the plume moves toward the pumping wells.

\section{Governing Equations}
Solute transport in the saturated zone follows advection and dispersion
with a first-order decay term.
We write the concentration field as
\begin{gather}
\dfrac{\partial c}{\partial t} = \nabla \cdot (D \nabla c) - \mathbf{v} \cdot \nabla c - k c \\
c(x, 0) = c_0(x), \qquad c|_{\partial \Omega} = c_b \\
\therefore M(t) = \int_{\Omega} c(x, t) \, dx
\end{gather}
where $D$ is the dispersion tensor and $k$ the decay constant.

\section{Monitoring}
Twelve wells are sampled monthly for nitrate and chloride.
The records span two full recharge cycles
and capture both irrigation seasons.

\section{Calibration}
The model is calibrated against the first year of concentrations
and validated on the second.
The dispersion length is the only adjusted parameter.

\section{Results}
The plume front advances about forty metres per year toward the wells,
and the decay term removes a fifth of the mass along the way.
At the current rate the nearest pumping well sees elevated nitrate
within six years.

\section{Conclusion}
Transport modelling puts a concrete clock on the nitrate risk
and gives the water board a defensible basis for land-use limits.
\end{document}

